 ## Background
-Sphere tracing was introduced by Turner Whitted in the context of ray tracing, but the specific geometric method for antialiased ray tracing of implicit surfaces using signed distance bounds was formalized by John C. Hart et al. in 1986 [S1]. The method was developed to provide a competitive alternative to standard ray tracing for implicit surfaces, particularly excelling at rendering sophisticated geometric modeling operations [S1]. Early implementations focused on basic sphere tracing, with subsequent enhancements like convexity and triangle inequality optimizations improving performance for complex scenes [S1]. The technique has since become a foundational method in procedural geometry rendering and is widely used in real-time graphics, particularly in shader-based rendering and implicit surface visualization [S2, unofficial].
+The term "sphere tracing" and the geometric method for antialiased ray tracing of implicit surfaces were introduced by Turner Whitted in [Kay & Kajiya, 1986] [S1]. The method was presented as a geometric alternative to standard ray tracing, particularly suited for implicit surfaces defined by distance fields. It gained prominence for its ability to handle complex geometric modeling operations and its inherent support for antialiasing through symbolic prefiltering [S1].
 
